## 10. Failure category discriminator

Every failure object emitted by the extraction layer, the resolver, or a downstream governance gate **must** carry a `failure_category` discriminator from the closed enumeration below. The enumeration is exhaustive for v0.4; adding a new value is a versioned change to this document and to the failure-object schema.

The `source_layer` column names which component produces the failure. The `retry_eligible` column is the routing rule for `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` and is enforced in code at the boundary of the extraction-retry cell.

| Category code | Source layer | Description | Retry-eligible |
|---|---|---|---|
| `EXTRACTION_DTO_MALFORMED` | Extraction/DTO | Intent DTO failed Pydantic validation | Yes |
| `EXTRACTION_FIELD_MISSING` | Extraction/DTO | A required intent field is absent | Yes |
| `EXTRACTION_ENUM_INVALID` | Extraction/DTO | An enum-typed intent field has a value outside the allowed set | Yes |
| `EXTRACTION_UNDERSPECIFIED` | Extraction/DTO | The extraction is internally inconsistent or under-specified in a way a re-phrasing could fix | Yes |
| `RESOLVER_UNKNOWN_ENTITY` | Resolver | A term resolves to no registry entity | No |
| `RESOLVER_UNKNOWN_COLUMN` | Resolver | A term resolves to no registry column on a known entity | No |
| `RESOLVER_AMBIGUOUS` | Resolver | A term resolves to more than one registry candidate | No |
| `RESOLVER_ALIAS_UNRESOLVED` | Resolver | An alias was provided but does not appear in the case-fold index | No |
| `RESOLVER_DISAMBIGUATION_REQUIRED` | Resolver | A case-fold collision exists at runtime that the registry has not disambiguated | No |
| `GOVERNANCE_POLICY_DENIED` | Governance | The policy engine denied the request | No |
| `GOVERNANCE_SENSITIVE_FIELD_BLOCKED` | Governance | A sensitive-field block applies | No |
| `GOVERNANCE_SCOPE_MISSING` | Governance | A required scope dimension has no value at request time | No |
| `GOVERNANCE_CREDENTIAL_MISMATCH` | Governance | No credential class satisfies the resolved access requirements | No |
| `GOVERNANCE_ROW_SCOPE_FAILED` | Governance | Row-scope predicate construction failed | No |

The retry cell **must** consume only failures with `retry_eligible: Yes`. Any failure with `retry_eligible: No` **must** be propagated terminally to the caller and to the audit ledger, regardless of runtime mode.

**Sanitized feedback rule per category.** The retry-cell template **must** emit the category code and, where applicable, the allowed-set. It **must not** emit the field name, the denied value, the registry candidates, or any reason text. The complete failure record — with field names, values, candidate sets, and reasons — goes to the audit ledger and is not visible to the LLM.

---

## 11. Compile-step signature

The compile step is the boundary between the Pydantic manifest and the frozen-dataclass compiled index. It is a pure function in the contract sense: given the same manifest and the same introspection snapshot, it **must** produce the same compiled index or the same compile-error set. It is owned by the deployment pipeline, not by the runtime resolver.

### Signature

```
compile(
    manifest: RegistryManifest,
    introspection: IntrospectionSnapshot,
    options: CompileOptions
) -> CompileResult
```

**Inputs.**

- `manifest: RegistryManifest` — the Pydantic-validated manifest as authored. v0.4 specifies the manifest's field inventory in §16; the full Pydantic schema is a separate implementation-spec pass.
- `introspection: IntrospectionSnapshot` — a captured snapshot of the live database schema at the time of compile. Treated as data, not as a live connection. The compile step does not query the database itself; it consumes a snapshot produced by the introspection step.
- `options: CompileOptions` — a small bundle of compile flags. v0.4 names two: `strict_drift` (default true; drift is treated as compile error rather than warning) and `disambiguation_required` (default true; case-fold collisions must be disambiguated explicitly, not silently broken).

**Output.**

- `CompileResult` is one of two shapes, discriminated by `status`:
  - `status: COMPILED` — carries a `CompiledRegistryIndex` (frozen dataclass + immutable maps; the resolver's runtime authority) and a `DriftReport` (see §12).
  - `status: COMPILE_FAILED` — carries an ordered list of `CompileError` objects and a `DriftReport`. No compiled index is produced.

**Properties.**

- The compile step **must** be deterministic: same manifest + same introspection snapshot + same options → same `CompileResult`.
- The compile step **must not** mutate its inputs.
- The compile step **must not** have side effects observable outside its return value, with the single exception of structured logging.
- A `CompiledRegistryIndex` once produced **must** be immutable for its lifetime. Loading a new registry version produces a new index; the old one is not modified.

**Failure modes** the compile step emits:

| Compile error | Trigger |
|---|---|
| `MANIFEST_VALIDATION_FAILED` | Pydantic validation of the manifest itself failed |
| `MANIFEST_DB_REFERENCE_BROKEN` | The manifest references an entity or column not present in the introspection snapshot |
| `CASE_FOLD_COLLISION` | Two manifest entries fold to the same canonical form with no explicit disambiguation |
| `SCOPE_DIMENSION_UNREACHABLE` | A declared scope dimension points to a column not present on the entity |
| `DRIFT_STRICT_VIOLATION` | `strict_drift=true` and the drift report contains any non-empty section |

---

## 12. Drift-report shape

The drift report is produced by the compile step (§11) on every invocation, regardless of whether the compile succeeded. It is the canonical record of how the manifest and the live database differ. It is consumed by registry authors, by the manifest-approval workflow, and by the audit ledger.

### Structure

```
DriftReport:
  snapshot_id: str            # identity of the introspection snapshot consumed
  snapshot_taken_at: datetime # when the snapshot was captured
  manifest_version: str       # identity of the manifest evaluated
  sections:
    missing_from_db: list[ManifestRef]
    missing_from_manifest: list[DbRef]
    type_mismatches: list[TypeMismatch]
    constraint_changes: list[ConstraintChange]
    nullable_changes: list[NullableChange]
```

Each section is independently populated. An empty section means no drift of that kind. A non-empty section under `strict_drift=true` is a compile error; under `strict_drift=false` it is a warning surfaced to the registry author.

**`missing_from_db`** lists manifest entries (entities or columns) that the introspection snapshot does not contain. These are broken references; the manifest cannot compile against this snapshot.

**`missing_from_manifest`** lists database entities or columns that exist in the snapshot but have no manifest entry. The resolver treats these as nonexistent regardless of compile mode. The list is surfaced so the registry author can decide whether to add the entries to the manifest or leave them out deliberately.

**`type_mismatches`** lists manifest entries whose declared type does not match the snapshot's type for the same identifier. Each entry names the manifest type, the snapshot type, and the identifier.

**`constraint_changes`** lists differences in primary-key, foreign-key, unique, or check constraints between manifest and snapshot.

**`nullable_changes`** lists columns whose nullability differs between manifest and snapshot.

### Authority

The drift report is informational, never authoritative. It **must not** be consulted by the resolver at request time. The compile step is the only consumer of the drift report; the resolver consumes only the compiled index.

---

## 13. Disambiguation declaration syntax

When two manifest entries case-fold to the same canonical form (§7), the manifest **must** declare an explicit disambiguation or the compile fails (§11). The disambiguation is registry-owned and is recorded in the compiled index.

### Declaration form

Disambiguation is declared on the colliding entries themselves. Each entry that participates in a collision **must** declare a `disambiguator` field whose value distinguishes it from its collision peers. The `disambiguator` value is opaque to the resolver — it is used only as a tiebreaker key — but it **must** be unique within the collision group.

```
entity: customer
  columns:
    - canonical: account_number
      aliases: ["acct no"]
      disambiguator: "long_form"
    - canonical: acct_no
      aliases: ["acct no"]
      disambiguator: "short_form"
```

With both entries declaring distinct disambiguators, the manifest compiles. The case-fold index records the folded form `"acct no"` as ambiguous-but-disambiguated; resolution of the term `"acct no"` from an extracted intent **must** then require the intent to carry the disambiguator value.

### Intent-side reference

When a registry term is disambiguated, the extracted-intent DTO **must** carry the disambiguator alongside the term:

```
extracted_intent:
  field:
    term: "acct no"
    disambiguator: "long_form"
```

The resolver matches the folded term *and* the disambiguator. If the disambiguator is absent or does not match any peer in the collision group, the resolution failure is `RESOLVER_DISAMBIGUATION_REQUIRED` (§10), which is terminal.

### Why the disambiguator is opaque

The disambiguator is not a display label, not an alias, and not user-facing. It is a registry-internal tag. Making it opaque prevents authoring affordances (decorators, builders) from accidentally surfacing it to the LLM. The LLM does not select the disambiguator; the calling layer does, on the basis of context the resolver does not own. Typical sources are tool configuration, use-case declaration, or the agent manifest.

### Scoping

Disambiguators **must** be unique within a collision group, not globally. Two unrelated entities may both use `"short_form"` as a disambiguator for unrelated case-fold collisions. The compile step verifies uniqueness per group, not across the manifest.

---

## 14. Test corpus shape

A v0.1 resolver implementation is conformant only if it passes a corpus that exercises every invariant from §2, every failure category from §10, every runtime mode from §3, and the case-fold and scope behaviors from §6 and §7. The corpus is not specified at the fixture level in v0.4 — that belongs to the implementation-spec pass — but the corpus *shape* is.

### Corpus organization

The corpus **must** be partitioned into five suites, each with its own pass/fail criteria:

| Suite | Purpose | Pass criterion |
|---|---|---|
| `closed_world` | Verifies the closed-world invariant | Every reference outside the registry produces a terminal failure of the correct category; no silent extension |
| `terminal_ambiguity` | Verifies the terminal-ambiguity invariant | Every multi-candidate match produces `RESOLVER_AMBIGUOUS`; no silent selection |
| `case_fold_edges` | Verifies Stage 1 matching against Unicode edge cases | Every fixture's expected fold result matches the resolver's actual fold; collisions trigger compile errors |
| `scope_mapping` | Verifies entity-level scope-dimension mapping | Every fixture's expected `resolved_scope` matches the resolver's output exactly |
| `retry_routing` | Verifies the source-layer-based retry routing | Every fixture's expected `retry_eligible` value matches the resolver's actual routing decision under `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` |

### Fixture form

Every fixture **must** carry:

- A registry manifest (or a reference to a shared one)
- An extracted-intent DTO
- The runtime mode under test
- The expected outcome: either a resolved-intent DTO or a typed failure with its category code

### Determinism check

The corpus **must** include a determinism suite that runs every other suite twice with identical inputs and asserts byte-identical outputs. This suite is the conformance check for the deterministic-authority invariant (§2). A resolver implementation that produces different outputs on a re-run is non-conformant regardless of how many other suites it passes.

### Coverage requirement

Every category code in §10 **must** appear in at least one fixture as an expected outcome. Every compile-error code in §11 **must** appear in at least one compile-error fixture. Every drift section in §12 **must** appear non-empty in at least one drift fixture.

---

## 15. Multi-entity resolved-intent structure

A single extracted intent often spans more than one entity — for example, a query that touches `customer` and `customer_order` together. The resolved-intent DTO **must** represent multi-entity resolution as a collection of independent per-entity resolutions, not as a single flat structure.

### Structure

```
ResolvedIntent:
  intent_id: str
  registry_version: str
  mode: RuntimeMode
  entities: list[ResolvedEntity]

ResolvedEntity:
  entity_canonical: str         # canonical name from the registry
  entity_physical: str          # physical name from the manifest, preserved verbatim
  fields: list[ResolvedField]
  scope: dict[DimensionName, PhysicalColumnName]

ResolvedField:
  field_canonical: str
  field_physical: str           # preserved verbatim
  role: FieldRole               # PROJECT | FILTER | GROUP | ORDER | etc.
```

The order of `entities` in the list reflects the order of mention in the extracted intent. It is preserved as metadata for downstream layers but **must not** be interpreted by the resolver as a join order or precedence. Join ordering is the query builder's responsibility.

### Per-entity independence

Each `ResolvedEntity` is the output of an independent application of the resolver's per-entity logic against the same compiled registry. The resolver **must not** allow cross-entity dependencies to affect the resolution of any single entity. If one entity fails to resolve, the resolver emits a typed failure naming that entity; it **must not** partially resolve other entities and emit a mixed result.

### Worked example

A user asks: *"Show recent orders and the email of the customer who placed each one."*

The extracted intent references two entities (`customer_order`, `customer`) and three fields (`customer_order.created_at`, `customer.email`, and implicitly the join key `customer.id` / `customer_order.customer_id`).

The resolved intent is:

```
ResolvedIntent:
  intent_id: "intent-001"
  registry_version: "registry-v17"
  mode: STRICT_DETERMINISTIC
  entities:
    - ResolvedEntity:
        entity_canonical: "customer_order"
        entity_physical: "CustomerOrder"
        fields:
          - field_canonical: "created_at"
            field_physical: "CreatedAt"
            role: ORDER
          - field_canonical: "customer_id"
            field_physical: "CustomerId"
            role: JOIN_KEY
        scope:
          tenant: "tenant_ref"
          region: "ord_region"
    - ResolvedEntity:
        entity_canonical: "customer"
        entity_physical: "Customer"
        fields:
          - field_canonical: "email"
            field_physical: "Email"
            role: PROJECT
          - field_canonical: "id"
            field_physical: "Id"
            role: JOIN_KEY
        scope:
          tenant: "tenant_id"
          region: "region"
```

The resolver has emitted two independent `ResolvedEntity` objects. Each has its own scope mapping. The query builder downstream constructs the actual join, projection, and scope predicates from this structure. The LLM has not seen any physical identifier; the scope columns are registry-owned per entity.

### Failure under multi-entity intent

If the LLM extracts a field that exists on one entity but not the other (e.g., a column `internal_notes` on `customer_order` that does not exist on `customer`), the failure is per-entity. The resolver emits `RESOLVER_UNKNOWN_COLUMN` naming the entity it attempted resolution against. There is no fallback to the other entity, no "best match" across entities, and no partial resolution returned alongside the failure.

---

## 16. Contract sketches — registry manifest and intent DTOs

This section names the field inventory of the three boundary contracts. It is the minimum sufficient to make the resolver's authority surface visible; it is not the full Pydantic schema. Concrete schemas with validators, computed fields, JSON Schema generation, and serialization aliases are a separate implementation-spec pass.

### Registry manifest — field inventory

```
RegistryManifest:
  version: str
  entities: list[ManifestEntity]
  shared_aliases: list[SharedAlias]            # optional; aliases that span entities
  scope_dimension_catalog: list[DimensionSpec] # canonical names for declared dimensions

ManifestEntity:
  canonical: str
  physical_name: str
  aliases: list[str]
  columns: list[ManifestColumn]
  scope_dimensions: dict[DimensionName, PhysicalColumnName]
  access_class: str                             # consumed by credential selector
  disambiguator: str | None                     # required if in a collision group

ManifestColumn:
  canonical: str
  physical_name: str
  type: ColumnType
  nullable: bool
  aliases: list[str]
  sensitive: bool                               # consumed by governance gates
  participates_in_dimension: DimensionName | None  # field-level scope refinement
  disambiguator: str | None
```

**What is sketched, not specified.** Validator functions, computed fields, deserialization rules, default values, and the precise enumeration of `ColumnType` are not in v0.4. The above is the field inventory; the implementation-spec pass specifies the types and validators.

### Extracted-intent DTO — field inventory

```
ExtractedIntent:
  intent_id: str
  source_query: str                             # the user's natural-language input
  requested_mode: RuntimeMode                   # subject to §3 activation rule
  entities: list[ExtractedEntityRef]
  fields: list[ExtractedFieldRef]

ExtractedEntityRef:
  term: str                                     # natural-language reference
  disambiguator: str | None                     # required if registry term is disambiguated

ExtractedFieldRef:
  entity_term: str                              # which entity this field belongs to
  field_term: str
  role: FieldRole
  disambiguator: str | None
```

**What is sketched, not specified.** The LLM extraction layer's full prompt contract, the relationship between `source_query` and the extracted fields, and the validation rules on `term` strings (length, character set, normalization) are not in v0.4.

### Resolved-intent DTO — field inventory

The resolved-intent DTO is already specified at structural level in §15. The field-level types defer to the implementation-spec pass.

### Boundary discipline

All three contracts are Pydantic v2 BaseModels at the boundary. The compiled-registry-index form, which the resolver consults at runtime, is a frozen-dataclass form derived from the manifest by the compile step (§11). The two are not interchangeable; the compile step is the only bridge.

---

## 17. Deferred sections

Three design areas remain out of scope for v0.4 and have their own design passes ahead.

**Two-tier registry — Platform and Tenant.** v0.4 treats the registry as a single compiled artifact. A future revision will split authority into a platform-owned base layer and tenant-owned override or extension entries, with explicit precedence and inheritance rules. The two-tier design needs the resolver scope to be stable first because the precedence rules are defined relative to the resolver's authority surface.

**Error model — raise versus return.** v0.4 specifies that the resolver emits typed Pydantic resolution-failure objects (§10). It does not yet specify, layer by layer, where these are raised as exceptions versus returned as values. The error-model pass will reconcile this against the retry boundary (extraction-repairable failures must be returnable, not raised), the audit ledger (every failure must be capturable regardless of raise/return), and API ergonomics (callers should not need a try/except for every resolution attempt).

**Approval workflow for `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY`.** v0.4 establishes that this mode is approval-gated at agent declaration time and that the runtime must downgrade or reject unapproved requests. It does not specify the approval workflow shape: who approves, what the approval artifact looks like, how it is bound to the agent manifest, how it is revoked, or how it interacts with the agent lifecycle. This is the largest of the three deferred items and will need to compose with the broader Ojas governance approval workflow.

---

## Appendix A — Resolver frozen principles (v0.4)

1. The resolver is a pure function of extracted intent and compiled registry.
2. Closed-world: no resolution outside the registry. Ever.
3. Terminal ambiguity: candidate sets greater than one fail; they do not collapse.
4. Candidate exposure splits three ways: full detail to audit, sanitized form to LLM, safe labels to user. Physical identifiers never reach the LLM or the user.
5. Deterministic authority: same inputs, same outputs, no LLM in the loop.
6. Retry never changes authority: every retry attempt is a fresh resolution.
7. `STRICT_DETERMINISTIC` is the default mode. `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` is the only approved exception, and the runtime must downgrade or reject unapproved requests for it.
8. Pydantic v2 at every boundary; frozen dataclasses internally; compile is the only bridge.
9. The manifest is the authoring authority; the compiled index is the runtime authority. The lifecycle is manifest → validation → introspection reconciliation → compile → approval → immutable index → runtime.
10. Database introspection validates and surfaces drift; it never extends the registry.
11. Scope dimensions are registered at the entity level. Use cases declare dimensions, not column names. The column-to-dimension mapping is never exposed to the LLM or the tool author.
12. Stage 1 matching is Unicode case-fold (NFKC + `casefold`); physical identifiers are preserved verbatim; collisions are compile-time errors and require explicit disambiguators.
13. Retry feedback is sanitized; runtime caps live outside retry count; every retry attempt is audited.
14. Resolver failures, governance-gate failures, and extraction-repairable failures originate from three different source layers and are routed by source layer, not by symptom.
15. The failure-category enumeration in §10 is closed; adding a new value is a versioned change to this document and to the failure-object schema.
16. The compile step is a pure function in the contract sense, owned by the deployment pipeline; same manifest + same introspection snapshot + same options must produce the same compiled index.
17. Disambiguators are registry-internal, opaque to the resolver, and never user-facing or LLM-facing.
18. Multi-entity resolution is per-entity-independent; the resolver must not allow cross-entity dependencies to affect single-entity resolution.

## Appendix B — Bounded sketches deferred to the implementation-spec pass

These two items are sketched at field-inventory level in §16 but deferred for full Pydantic schemas:

- Pydantic registry manifest schema (full validator and computed-field specification)
- Extracted-intent DTO and resolved-intent DTO schemas (full type, validator, and serialization specification)

The implementation-spec pass will produce the concrete Pydantic classes consistent with the field inventories in §16.

## Appendix C — Conformance assertions for v0.1

The assertions below are the minimum behavioral conformance set for the resolver. A v0.1 implementation that passes all of these has demonstrated the invariants in §2 are enforced in code, not just in prose. Each assertion is keyed to the section that specifies the behavior.

| # | Assertion | Spec ref | Expected result |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | DB column exists but manifest omits it | §5, §12 | Does not resolve; reported as `missing_from_manifest` drift |
| 2 | Manifest entry has no matching DB column | §11 | `MANIFEST_DB_REFERENCE_BROKEN` at compile time |
| 3 | Alias maps to two columns in the same entity without explicit disambiguation | §7, §13 | `CASE_FOLD_COLLISION` at compile time |
| 4 | Two columns differ only in case without disambiguation | §7, §13 | `CASE_FOLD_COLLISION` at compile time |
| 5 | Same input + same registry version, run twice | §2, §14 | Byte-identical output (determinism suite) |
| 6 | Resolver-layer failure under `STRICT_DETERMINISTIC` | §3, §8 | Terminal; no LLM retry |
| 7 | Resolver-layer failure under `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` | §8, §10 | Terminal; LLM not invoked for retry |
| 8 | `EXTRACTION_*` failure under `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` | §8, §10 | Retry-eligible; sanitized feedback emitted |
| 9 | `GOVERNANCE_*` failure under `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` | §8, §10 | Terminal; not a resolver failure |
| 10 | Case-fold alias match (`"Customer Name"` → `customer name`) | §7 | Resolves to canonical entry; physical identifier preserved |
| 11 | Physical quoted identifier (`"CustomerName"`) | §7 | Preserved exactly in resolved output |
| 12 | Unapproved caller requests `GOVERNED_EXTRACTION_RETRY` | §3 | Downgraded to `STRICT_DETERMINISTIC` or rejected |
| 13 | Retry feedback after `EXTRACTION_ENUM_INVALID` | §8, §10 | Contains category code and allowed-set only; never the invalid value, never the denied field name |
| 14 | Two consecutive retry attempts with same `(failure_category, failed_field_term)` | §8 | Request terminates on no-progress detection |
| 15 | Use case declares `scope_columns = [...]` (column names) | §6 | Resolver refuses the declaration |
| 16 | Use case declares `scope_dimensions = [...]` (dimension names) | §6 | Resolver maps each to the registry-owned column on each resolved entity |
| 17 | Disambiguated registry term resolved with matching disambiguator | §13 | Resolves to the disambiguated entry |
| 18 | Disambiguated registry term resolved with absent or mismatched disambiguator | §13 | `RESOLVER_DISAMBIGUATION_REQUIRED`; terminal |
| 19 | Multi-entity intent with one resolving and one failing | §15 | Per-entity failure; no partial resolution emitted |
| 20 | Drift report sections empty under `strict_drift=true` | §11, §12 | Compile succeeds |
| 21 | Drift report has any non-empty section under `strict_drift=true` | §11, §12 | `DRIFT_STRICT_VIOLATION` at compile time |

---

**Document status:** Freeze Candidate v0.2. The resolver's authority surface, invariants, and behavioral contract are stable. Three architectural sections remain deferred (two-tier registry, error model, approval workflow). Two boundary contracts remain sketched at field-inventory level pending the implementation-spec pass (registry manifest schema, extracted/resolved-intent DTO schemas). Promotion to final freeze depends on either: (a) settling at least one of the three deferred architectural sections, or (b) producing the implementation-spec pass that closes the two bounded sketches — and the design history showing one path or the other was chosen deliberately.
